Microsoft Learn: Create, Configure, and Manage Users in Microsoft Entra ID

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Create, Configure, and Manage Users
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to create, configure, and manage user accounts in Microsoft Entra ID to control authentication and access to Azure resources.

This module focused on understanding different user identity types, managing users through the Microsoft Entra Admin Center, and learning how authentication and authorization work within Azure environments.

Step 1: Understanding User Accounts in Microsoft Entra ID

The module introduced Microsoft Entra ID user accounts as the foundation of identity and access management in Azure.

Every user who requires access to Azure resources must have a user account. During sign-in, Microsoft Entra ID authenticates the user and generates an access token that determines:

Which resources the user can access
What actions the user can perform
What permissions are assigned

This authentication and authorization process helps secure Azure environments and enforce access controls.

Step 2: Managing Users in the Microsoft Entra Admin Center

The training demonstrated how administrators manage users through the Microsoft Entra Admin Center.

Skills practiced:
Viewing user accounts
Managing user properties
Identifying user types
Navigating Azure directories
Switching between directories and subscriptions
Administrative features:
Feature	Purpose
All Users	View and manage user accounts
Directory Switching	Change between Microsoft Entra directories
User Management	Create, modify, and remove users
User Type Identification	Distinguish members and guest accounts

The module emphasized that administrators can only manage one directory at a time and must switch directories when working across multiple tenants.

Step 3: Cloud Identities

The module introduced cloud-only identities.

Characteristics:
Exist only within Microsoft Entra ID
Managed directly in Azure
Not synchronized from on-premises environments
Examples:
Azure administrators
Cloud-only employees
Service-specific administrative accounts
Key concepts learned:
Source is Microsoft Entra ID
Can be created and managed directly in the cloud
Removed permanently when deleted from the directory

Cloud identities are commonly used in cloud-native organizations without traditional on-premises Active Directory infrastructure.

Step 4: Directory-Synchronized Identities

The training covered hybrid identity management using synchronized accounts.

Characteristics:
Created in on-premises Active Directory
Synchronized into Microsoft Entra ID
Allow users to maintain a single identity across environments
Synchronization tools:
Tool	Purpose
Microsoft Entra Cloud Sync	Recommended for most organizations
Microsoft Entra Connect Sync	Used for complex synchronization scenarios
Key lessons learned:
Source is Windows Server Active Directory
Supports hybrid cloud deployments
Enables centralized identity management
Maintains consistency between on-premises and cloud environments

Organizations with existing Active Directory infrastructure commonly use synchronized identities.

Step 5: Guest Users

The module introduced guest users for external collaboration.

Examples:
Contractors
Vendors
Business partners
External consultants
Characteristics:
Exist outside the organization
Invited into the tenant
Granted limited access to specific resources
Benefits:
Secure external collaboration
Controlled resource access
Temporary permissions
Easy account removal when access is no longer required

Guest accounts help organizations work securely with external users without creating internal employee accounts.

Step 6: Authentication and Authorization

A major focus of the module was understanding how Microsoft Entra ID manages access.

Authentication

Authentication verifies a user's identity.

Example:

Username
Password
Multifactor Authentication (MFA)
Authorization

Authorization determines what a user can do after authentication succeeds.

Examples:

Access Azure resources
Read storage accounts
Manage virtual machines
Modify subscriptions
Process Flow
User signs in.
Microsoft Entra ID validates credentials.
An access token is generated.
Permissions are evaluated.
Access is granted or denied.

This process ensures secure access management throughout Azure environments.

